    "description": "Develop software for OpenGL based software tool kit\n\nCurrently, Scrum Master of an eight person team tasked with improving the performance of rendering engine.  Ensured that team member stayed on track for their deliverables and that stakeholders are aware of the progress of the deliverables.  Prototyped the following and assisted team with implementation: \nImproved light point rendering by x5\nImproved tree (batch) rendering performance by x3-4\nEliminated draw spikes due texture allocations.  \nUpgraded OpenGL version used by rendering engine to OpenGL 3.1\nAdded support for AMD/ATI and Intel GPUs\n\nSubject Matter Expert of rendering engine, and supporting technology - OpenGL, GLSL shaders.  Provided design advice to visualization team for all rendering features.  Provided estimates for new features and prototype approaches.\n\nImplemented dynamic shadows.\nVideo : http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-IE9Ie7F6ao \nWhite paper: http://www.presagis.com/files/whitepapers/Shadow_Mapping_in_High_Performance_Visual_Simulation-FINAL.pdf\n\nImplemented a morphing terrain mesh rendering approach to improve cull performance as well as rendering performance of multiple LOD terrain system (Morphing Smart Mesh) in a cross product team.  This was a high profile features as it was a large part of the company’s vision for all products to work together in a suite.\n\nWas part of the team that develop an infrastructure that auto generated shaders based on OpenGL state and optional shader techniques.  Responsibilities include the design and implementation of the weaver, the portion of the system that merged the individual pieces of the shader together.  Using this infrastructure, I developed shader techniques for bump, light, alpha, light materials, and reflection maps. White paper: \nhttp://www.presagis.com/resources/whitepapers/automated_shader_generation_using_a_shader_infrastructure/",
